// 'length' reports how much an array or a string holds, so a write to it is refused, on an array
// a null check narrowed as on any other. A '?.' write reports its own refusal beside the member's,
// and with no advice, because checking for null would not make that write work either.
// @expect-error 'maybe.length' is the number of elements in 'maybe', so it cannot be assigned. An array keeps the length it was created with. Create a new array of the size you need, as in 'new int[...]'.
// @expect-error '?.' cannot appear on the left side of '=', because there is nothing to write to when 'maybe' is null.
// @expect-error '?.' cannot appear on the left side of '+=', because there is nothing to write to when 'label' is null.
// @expect-error build failed with 5 problems

main() -> int {
    int[]? maybe = new int[2];
    if (maybe != null) {
        maybe.length = 3;
    }
    maybe?.length = 3;
    string? label = "ab";
    label?.length += 1;
    return 0;
}
